 ## Overview
-Empty-space skipping (ESS) is a volume rendering acceleration technique that reduces computational cost by identifying and bypassing regions of the volumetric dataset that are transparent or contain no relevant data [S1, S2, S6, unofficial]. It solves the problem of wasted computation when ray marching through empty space, which constitutes a significant portion of the rendering time in sparse volumes [S3, S5, unofficial]. Unlike [Early Stop](1790-early-stop.md), which terminates rays based on accumulated opacity, ESS proactively skips intervals of the ray path before sampling, often using auxiliary data structures like octrees, bounding volumes, or occupancy grids [S2, S6, S8, unofficial].
+Empty-space skipping (ESS) is a volume rendering acceleration technique that reduces computational cost by identifying and bypassing regions of the volumetric dataset that are transparent or contain no relevant data [S1, S2, S6, unofficial]. It solves the problem of wasted computation when ray marching through empty space, which constitutes a significant portion of the rendering time in sparse volumes [S3, S5, unofficial]. Unlike [Early Stop](1790-early-stop.md), which terminates rays based on accumulated opacity, ESS proactively skips intervals of the ray path before sampling, often using auxiliary data structures like octrees, bounding volumes, or occupancy grids [S2, S6, S8, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from [Bounding Volume Culling](1239-bounding-volume-culling.md) by operating on the internal structure of the volume data rather than just external object bounds [S6, unofficial].
 
